In this lesson, we'll look at refining a cubic root when the numbers don't factor nicely. Here's the big idea. Every cubic has at least one rational root hiding in plain sight, and the rational root theorem hands you a short list of candidates to test. Once you spot that root, you slice the cubic with it, leaving a smaller quadratic that you then tackle with the usual tools — factorising, completing the square, or the quadratic formula. So far, so Unit 1. The twist for Methods comes when the quadratic fights back. The discriminant tells you what you're up against: positive means two real roots, zero means one repeated root, negative means they're complex and that's where we stop numerically. And here's the key move — when the quadratic does have real roots but they come out as ugly nested square roots, that's your cue to bring in Newton's method. You take a quick sketch or sign chart to pick a sensible starting value, then iterate Newton's update and watch the decimal answers snap into place. Think of it as polishing what algebra started. Now let's see it in action.